Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 19:40:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] tp: 0022 root-cause the batched-verify "token 0 depends on K"
 anomaly, and re-measure m so the slope means something

APPLY WITH: git apply --ignore-whitespace 0022-*.diff
(verified on the fleet: reproduces the built source byte-for-byte modulo line
endings. Plain `git apply` and GNU `patch` both refuse, because
runtime/src/models/kimi_k3_dist_forward.cpp carries CRLF in this working tree
while the rest of it is LF -- a pre-existing condition, not introduced here.
Patch 0021 is itself stored CRLF and has the same problem; worth normalising the
series before it is pushed.)

DIAGNOSTIC ONLY. The engine change is inert unless SPARKINFER_K3_AR_PAD is set;
with it unset this binary reproduced patch 0021's measured numbers to all nine
printed digits (K=2 pos=0 cos=0.998947945, max|d|=5.615e-01), which is the
inertness proof. The rest is --spec-bench instrumentation.

=========================== PART 1: THE ANOMALY ===========================

0021's --spec-bench reported that token 0's logits from
kimi_k3_dist_forward_batch(K) changed with K -- cos vs the sequential
single-token reference 0.998948 at K=2, 0.998871 at K=3, 0.998446 at K=4 and
flat thereafter. Token 0's correct output cannot depend on how many tokens
follow it, so this was either cross-token contamination (a defect) or a
shape-driven re-association (numerics). 0021 tried to separate the two with
SPARKINFER_K3_MOE_BATCH=0, got identical results either way, and recorded that
as inconclusive on the theory that the expert-major regroup might be declining
below its own token floor at K <= 8 and never running in either arm.

THAT THEORY IS WRONG AND THE TEST WAS VALID. The regroup's floor is
k3_moe_batch_min_tok(), which defaults to 2 -- "every batched call"
(k3_kernels.cu:5148-5161, gate at :5265). K=2..8 clears it, and the rest of the
contract passes at this geometry (IQ1_S is ggml_type 19, accepted at :5268;
latent 3584 % 256 == 0 and ffn 1536 % 256 == 0 at :5267). The regroup WAS
engaged, MOE_BATCH=0 genuinely switched it off, and the two agreeing exonerates
it rather than proving nothing.

TWO PROBES ADDED, AND WHAT THEY ESTABLISHED.

1. FILLER INVARIANCE. Hold K fixed, hold token 0 fixed, replace tokens 1..K-1
   with far out-of-distribution ids (they route to different experts and drive
   different KDA state), compare token 0 BITWISE. Data contamination moves
   token 0; a shape effect cannot.

     MEASURED, TP3, K = 2..6, every K:  BIT-IDENTICAL, max|d| = 0.000e+00
   across all 163840 logits. No value belonging to any later token reaches
   token 0. THE CONTAMINATION HYPOTHESIS IS DEAD.

2. SPARKINFER_K3_AR_PAD=N pins every all-reduce to N rows regardless of the
   live row count, zeroing the padding. ncclAllReduce is deterministic for a
   FIXED count/communicator/topology, but its per-element cross-rank summation
   order is a function of THE COUNT: reduce-scatter partitions the buffer, the
   partition owner is where that element's 3-way sum starts, and which rank owns
   a given element moves when the count moves. A K-token batch reduces width*K,
   so token 0's elements land in a different partition for every K -- (a+b)+c
   becomes (b+c)+a for some of them, 185 collectives per pass.

     MEASURED, TP3, SPARKINFER_K3_AR_PAD=8, K = 2..6:
       K=2..6 pos=0   cos=1.000000000  max|d|=0.000e+00  exact=100.0000%
   Bit-identical across every K AND to the sequential single-token path.
   Unpinned it was exact=0.0000% at every K.

CONCLUSION: THE BATCHED KERNELS ARE EXACT; THE COLLECTIVE IS NOT COUNT-STABLE.
Every bit-identity claim 0021 leans on holds -- the batched KDA q/k/v/g group,
the expert-major regroup, moe_router_shared_kernel standing in for the register
router at n_tok > 1, proj_f16_tok_kernel for the head. None contributes a single
differing bit. Independently confirmed with a collective-free control:
kimi_k3_kda_batch_check tp=1, layers 0..15, phase All, K=2..6, all bit-identical.

The amplifier is the one the design doc already named (§9.2): with 896 experts
at IQ1_S the rank-4-to-16 routing tail is a near-tie lottery, so a last-bit
difference in a residual flips expert selection and the logits move by O(1).

WHAT STILL DIFFERS UNDER THE PIN, AND WHY IT CONFIRMS THE MECHANISM. Positions
b >= 1 remain inexact (cos 0.980-0.992) because the pin fixes the COUNT but not
the OFFSET: token b's partial sits at row b in the batched arm and at row 0 in
the sequential arm, so it still lands in a different partition. Token 0 is the
one row whose offset is 0 in both arms, and it is exactly the row that goes
bit-exact. Position b's result is now a function of b alone and no longer of K,
which is the causal property the anomaly violated.

THE PIN IS A PROBE, NOT A FIX. It costs bandwidth and does not make b >= 1
exact. The real fix, if bit-exact speculative verification is wanted, is a
count- and offset-invariant collective -- an allgather of each rank's partial
plus a local sum in fixed rank order, bit-identical by construction. These
payloads are latency-bound at 28.7 KB (attn) and 43.0 KB (moe) per token, so 3x
the bytes may cost little; worth measuring before it is designed.

======================= PART 2: MEASURING m PROPERLY =======================

0021 fitted step(K) = T + m*K from 5 samples per K and reported m = 37-39 ms,
with K=3 measuring SLOWER than K=4. Two defects in that protocol, both of which
push directly on the slope:

  CONFOUND. All reps of K=1 ran, then all of K=2, and so on, so K was perfectly
  correlated with wall-clock time and any slow drift landed in the SLOPE rather
  than the noise. Fixed by sweeping: one sample of every K per sweep, with the
  starting K rotated per sweep so no K keeps a favoured slot. Rotation is by
  sweep index, not random, because every rank must issue the identical call
  sequence or the collectives will not match.

  MEAN OF FIVE. Reported as a median now, with p25/p75/min/mean/sd printed, plus
  se(m), a Student-t 95% CI at n-2 dof, R2, and a Theil-Sen robust cross-check.

RESULT: THE NON-MONOTONICITY IS REAL, NOT NOISE. At 21 rotated sweeps the
medians are tight (IQR 1-3 ms) and the inversions survive with DISJOINT IQRs:

  K   median     p25      p75      min      (TP3, KDA_FUSE=0, PROJ_TOKS=8)
  1   164.49   164.35   165.84   163.39
  2   194.55   193.66   196.02   192.73
  3   289.73   289.24   290.69   288.20
  4   236.09   234.74   236.54   233.52     <- 54 ms FASTER than K=3
  5   332.07   331.64   335.75   328.64
  6   354.75   353.21   356.60   350.40
  7   376.62   374.89   380.17   372.68
  8   320.44   319.12   323.70   315.37     <- 56 ms FASTER than K=7

step(K) IS A SAWTOOTH, NOT A LINE, so a single OLS slope over K=1..8 is not an
estimate of marginal cost -- it reports 27-37 ms depending on which K it sees,
and its R2 of 0.75 is measuring the sawtooth, not scatter. THAT is what the
"m = 37 ms" number was.

The low branch is K = 2, 4, 8, and it is very nearly a perfect line:

  step(K) = 152.3 + 20.9*K ms      R2 = 0.9999
  m = 20.9 ms   95% CI [18.6, 23.3]   (dof = 1; the CI is wide because n = 3,
  the POINT estimate is not: the three independent spans give 20.40 (K=2->4),
  21.15 (K=4->8) and 20.90 (K=2->8) ms/token, agreeing within 0.8 ms, and the
  K=2 and K=4 medians reproduce to +-0.3 ms across four separate fleet runs.)

The high branch sits a near-constant ~75 ms above that line (K=3: +74.8,
K=5: +74.5, K=6: +76.5, K=7: +77.8), i.e. roughly one extra full pass over the
batched projection weights.

PARTIALLY EXPLAINED, AND THE REST IS STATED RATHER THAN GUESSED. A direct A/B
on SPARKINFER_K3_PROJ_TOKS at K=8 moves step from 385.58 ms (env 4, TOKS=4,
tok_blocks=2) to 320.44 ms (env 8, TOKS=8, tok_blocks=1) -- a 65 ms swing that
confirms ceil(n_tok/TOKS) extra passes over the projection weights are a real
and large cost. But widening the tile-selection rule in
k3_proj_q8act_tok_f32/k3_proj_f16_tok_f32 so that K=3,5,6,7 also get
tok_blocks=1 did NOT move their timings at all (rebuilt, redeployed md5-verified
to all three ranks, re-measured: 289.73/332.07/354.75/376.62 vs
289.51/331.67/352.70/375.46 before -- unchanged within noise). That change was
therefore REVERTED rather than shipped, and the ~75 ms penalty at K=3,5,6,7
remains UNEXPLAINED. It is a known-unknown in the cost model.

CONSEQUENCE FOR THE GATE. m = 20.9 ms at the usable K values, against the design
doc's "materially above ~20 ms => stop" rule (§8). The gate is met, marginally.
Break-even acceptance m/T = 20.9/164.5 = 0.127, against the design's modelled
0.093. But K is not freely choosable while the sawtooth stands: only
K in {2,4,8} (drafts N in {1,3,7}) sit on the low branch, which removes the
adaptive-N strategy §5.4 recommends. Recomputed §5.3 requirements at the three
usable depths: 8 tok/s needs p ~ 0.50, 10 tok/s p ~ 0.65, 12 tok/s p ~ 0.77,
15 tok/s p ~ 0.85 -- each 0.07-0.12 higher than the design's table.

